PRECISION-FLOOR SURVEY, registered in experiments/prereg_floor_survey.json (sha256 05bc8157207c..., frozen before any new twin). Each model measured at float32 and bfloat16 on the frozen probe set, same estimator and same tau ladder as F185.  FLOOR AT THE CONTESTED RUNG (tau=0.5): gpt-neo-1.3B 1.0 (n=982); gpt-neo-125m 1.0 (n=1175); pythia-160m 1.0 [reused]; pythia-1b 1.0 (n=1525); pythia-410m 0.995 [reused]; pythia-70m 1.0 (n=254), NOT DECIDABLE; rwkv-4-430m-pile 1.0 (n=1289); mamba-130m-hf 1.0 (n=1160).  KF1 FIRES -- THE TAU=0.5 RUNG IS RETIRED. Every floor in the cohort is at or above 0.99 at that rung, so the resolution ratio has no stable denominator anywhere. F185's 11.76x may never be quoted, and this is a stronger retirement than the replication's NOT DECIDABLE: the rung is not merely unreplicated on one pair, it is unresolvable on this estimand. Registered as the expected outcome before the six new floors were measured.  AND THE SPREAD F187 FOUND, now measured across the cohort: at tau=0 the floors run 0.201 to 0.9778 across 6 models. Both earlier preregs treated one model's floor as THE floor; it is not, and every per-model robustness statement in the paper rests on this spread.  REFUSALS: no p-value; no adjustment of the 0.99 bound, the ladder, or the source floor; no replication run here even under KF2; no claim that a bfloat16 floor bounds a quantized one -- 4-bit remains fatal per F187.

